<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Venicones</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of Fife and the Tay region recorded by Ptolemy, whose lands later formed a core region of the Picts.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Venicones appear in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Ptolemy's Geography</a> of about 150, generally placed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a> and perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinross-shire">Kinross</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angus">Angus</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Forth</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tay">Tay</a>, with a settlement called Orrea. They descended from local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> communities living in hillforts, crannogs and open farmsteads on fertile lowland soils.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nothing is recorded of their rulers or institutions. Their territory lay close to the Roman frontier: forts along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gask-ridge">Gask Ridge</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpow">Carpow</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tay">Tay</a>, built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Severus</a> around 208, show heavy Roman activity. Some historians think they are the people later called <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/maeatae">Maeatae</a>, or part of them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Venicones disappear from the written record after Ptolemy. Their lands later formed the Pictish province of Fib, which gave its name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a>, within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/venicones.jpg" alt="Venicones" width="320" height="464"><figcaption>Image: (myself) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain.north.peoples.Ptolemy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic (Pictish)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=venicones" aria-label="Venicones on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4625 276 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Venicones lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4945 532h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4945 532h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United Kingdom. <a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=venicones">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

